<Application
    x:Class="MCPRouter.Shell.App"
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
    <Application.Resources>
        <ResourceDictionary>
            <ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>
                <!--
                  The Fluent control resources and nothing else. Every colour this shell draws is
                  a WinUI theme resource by key (DESIGN.md §12's provenance rule), so there is no
                  app palette to merge: the tokens are the platform's, and a hand-authored copy of
                  them would be a second palette to keep in step with the first.
                -->
                <XamlControlsResources xmlns="using:Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Controls" />
            </ResourceDictionary.MergedDictionaries>

            <!--
              `SubtleButtonStyle` is NOT a WinUI 3 key. Eleven references across five views asked
              for it - CleanupBoard, InboxBoard, HarnessesBoard, AmbientFlyoutView and
              CapabilityDocumentPanel - and every one of them threw at runtime with
              "Cannot find a resource with the given key: SubtleButtonStyle". The XAML ones
              surfaced as `XamlParseException: undeclared prefix`, which names neither the key nor
              the file and is why this took a stack trace to find (DEF-078).

              It cost three of the nine boards. Cleanup and Inbox threw inside
              InitializeComponent, Harnesses threw in its constructor's card builder, and because
              ShowDestination mounted the board in the same statement that set the window title,
              the shell simply kept showing the previous board. The two dialog surfaces were never
              opened by anything, so they had not failed yet - they would have.

              Defined here rather than removed from the call sites: a subtle button is what the
              design of record asks for in all five places, and eleven deletions would be
              answering a missing definition by dropping the intent. Based on the platform's own
              DefaultButtonStyle so it inherits every state, focus visual and theme response
              rather than reimplementing them.
            -->
            <Style x:Key="SubtleButtonStyle" TargetType="Button" BasedOn="{StaticResource DefaultButtonStyle}">
                <Setter Property="Background" Value="{ThemeResource SubtleFillColorTransparentBrush}" />
                <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{ThemeResource ControlFillColorTransparentBrush}" />
            </Style>
        </ResourceDictionary>
    </Application.Resources>
</Application>
